## What is Hydra?

Hydra is a command line tool designed to automate authentication attempts against supported services.

Instead of manually trying different credentials, Hydra can take a list of possible usernames and passwords and test them systematically.

A simplified workflow looks like:

```text
Username List
      +
Password List
      ↓
    Hydra
      ↓
Authentication Attempts
      ↓
Target Service
      ↓
Response Analysis
```

Hydra is particularly useful during authorised penetration tests when assessing whether a service is vulnerable to weak or easily guessable credentials.

## Authentication Attacks

Authentication is the process of verifying who a user is.

A typical login system may require:

```text
Username + Password
```

If an application or service allows unlimited login attempts without adequate protection, an attacker may repeatedly try different credentials.

Hydra can automate this process.

## Brute Force vs Password Spraying

### Brute Force

A brute force attack attempts many possible passwords against an account or service.

The password space can become extremely large depending on the possible characters and password length.

### Password Attack Using Wordlists

Instead of generating every possible combination, a tester can use a wordlist containing commonly used or previously discovered passwords.

This can significantly reduce the number of attempts required.

### Password Spraying

Password spraying uses a small number of commonly used passwords against many different usernames.

The goal is to avoid repeatedly attacking a single account.

These techniques have different characteristics and should only be performed against systems where testing is authorised.

## Wordlists

Wordlists are an important part of password testing.

A wordlist is simply a file containing candidate values, usually one per line.

For example:

```text
password
admin
welcome
letmein
example123
```

During a Hydra attack, Hydra can read candidate usernames or passwords from these files.

Common wordlist locations on security focused Linux distributions may include:

```text
/usr/share/wordlists/
```

The quality of a wordlist can significantly affect the effectiveness and speed of password testing.

## Basic Hydra Syntax

A general Hydra command follows a structure similar to:

```bash
hydra [options] [target] [service]
```

When username and password wordlists are involved, additional options specify where the candidate credentials should come from.

A generic example is:

```bash
hydra -l username -P passwords.txt target.example ssh
```

Here:

`-l` specifies a single username.

`-P` specifies a password wordlist.

The target identifies the host being tested.

The final argument identifies the service or protocol.

The exact syntax depends on the service being tested.

## Username Options

Hydra provides different options for supplying usernames.

### Single Username

A single username can be specified with:

```bash
-l username
```

### Username List

A file containing multiple usernames can be supplied using:

```bash
-L usernames.txt
```

This allows Hydra to test multiple possible accounts.

## Password Options

Passwords can similarly be provided in different ways.

### Single Password

A single password can be supplied with:

```bash
-p password
```

### Password List

A password wordlist can be supplied with:

```bash
-P passwords.txt
```

The uppercase and lowercase options are important because they perform different functions.

## Hydra Modules

Hydra supports many different protocols and services.

Examples include:

```text
SSH
FTP
HTTP
HTTPS
SMB
SMTP
RDP
Telnet
MySQL
PostgreSQL
```

Hydra uses modules to communicate with different services.

The correct module must be selected according to the authentication service being tested.

## SSH

SSH provides remote command line access to a system.

A generic Hydra example for an authorised SSH assessment is:

```bash
hydra -l username -P passwords.txt target.example ssh
```

Hydra attempts the supplied credentials against the SSH service and analyses the responses.

## FTP

FTP is another network service that can provide username and password authentication.

A generic example is:

```bash
hydra -l username -P passwords.txt target.example ftp
```

The same general concept applies, but Hydra uses the FTP protocol module.

## Web Login Forms

Hydra can also interact with certain HTTP based login forms.

Web form attacks are more complex because Hydra needs to understand:

• The login endpoint

• Username parameter

• Password parameter

• HTTP method

• Failure or success condition

A generic structure can look like:

```bash
hydra target.example http-post-form "login_path:parameters:failure_condition"
```

The exact syntax depends on how the web application implements authentication.

## Understanding Hydra Output

Hydra provides information about the progress and results of an attack.

A successful credential pair may be reported in a format similar to:

```text
[service] host: target
login: username
password: password
```

When analysing output, pay attention to:

• Target host

• Service

• Username

• Password

• Number of attempts

• Connection information

It is important to verify discovered credentials rather than assuming every reported result is valid.

## Useful Options

Hydra provides many command line options.

Some commonly encountered options include:

```text
-l    Single username
-L    Username wordlist
-p    Single password
-P    Password wordlist
-s    Specify a port
-v    Verbose output
-V    Show individual login attempts
-t    Number of parallel tasks
-f    Stop after finding a valid credential
-h    Display help
```

Options can be combined depending on the requirements of the assessment.

## Performance

Hydra can perform multiple authentication attempts concurrently.

The number of parallel tasks can be controlled using:

```bash
-t
```

Increasing the number of tasks can improve speed, but it can also:

• Increase network traffic

• Put additional load on the target

• Trigger security controls

• Cause account lockouts

Therefore, concurrency should be configured carefully during authorised testing.

## Hydra and Network Services

Before using Hydra, it is important to understand what services are actually exposed by the target.

A typical authorised assessment may look like:

```text
Reconnaissance
      ↓
Identify Open Services
      ↓
Identify Authentication Mechanism
      ↓
Choose Appropriate Hydra Module
      ↓
Select Wordlists
      ↓
Perform Controlled Testing
      ↓
Validate Results
```

This demonstrates why Hydra is normally used after basic reconnaissance rather than blindly targeting services.

## Security Defences

Hydra demonstrates why authentication protections are important.

Applications and services can reduce password attack risk through:

### Rate Limiting

Restricting how quickly authentication attempts can be made.

### Account Lockout

Temporarily locking an account after repeated failed attempts.

### Multi Factor Authentication

Requiring an additional authentication factor beyond a password.

### Strong Password Policies

Encouraging or enforcing sufficiently strong and unique passwords.

### Monitoring

Logging and detecting unusual authentication activity.

### CAPTCHA

Using challenges to reduce automated authentication attempts in appropriate situations.

## Security Relevance

Hydra is useful for demonstrating the risks associated with weak authentication.

A service may be exposed to password attacks when it has:

• Weak passwords

• Reused credentials

• No rate limiting

• Poor account lockout controls

• Missing multi factor authentication

• Exposed authentication services

Hydra helps security testers evaluate these weaknesses in controlled environments.

